# Line endings are LF, in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# Every tracked text file is already LF in the index - `git ls-files --eol`
# reports i/lf for all 377 of them and i/crlf for none. So this file does not
# change any stored content; it fixes what CHECKOUT produces.
#
# Without it, `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default, and set on at least one
# machine here) rewrites files to CRLF on the way out. Several tests assert on
# the source text of the modules they cover, matching across line boundaries,
# and those matches fail against CRLF. A fresh Windows clone would therefore
# fail four tests that pass everywhere else - a failure caused by the checkout
# rather than by any change to the code.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides core.autocrlf for this repository, so every clone gets the
# same bytes regardless of the developer's global git configuration.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files git must never touch. It already auto-detects these, but saying
# so explicitly means a corrupted image can never be blamed on eol handling.
*.png binary
*.svg text eol=lf
